In late 2091, as the black gulf tides swallowed the lowlands of Old Atlanta, the survivor-scavengers known as "Float-Pickers" wired stolen satellite transceivers directly into aluminum vibraphones and salvaged gut-string guitars. Perched high above the rising sea on the rusted industrial rooftops of the high-rise slums, they hosted midnight "Silt-Dances." Attire was a surreal mashup of pressure-sealed deep-sea diving suits, oil-stained leather fringe, and reflective silvered Stetson hats built from heat-shield foil. The performance ritual centered around "dousing the vibe"—pouring brackish floodwater over the vibraphone's spinning aluminum bars during solos to create violently pitched, bubbling acoustic bends while the crowd swayed in magnetic lock-boots, staring up at the neon-choked sky.

